 ### Gaussian Densification via Rendering Loss
-A strategy to add new 3D Gaussians to the map by identifying unobserved regions (holes) and regions with high rendering error, using opacity, color, and depth thresholds [S3]. It solves the problem of incomplete reconstruction in SLAM systems by actively densifying areas that are not well-represented by the current Gaussian set [S3]. It sets itself apart from gradient-based densification by directly using rendering metrics (opacity and error) to guide densification [S3].
+A strategy to add new 3D Gaussians to the map by identifying unobserved regions (holes) and regions with high rendering error, using opacity, color, and depth thresholds [S6]. It identifies holes by checking if rendered opacity $O = \sum_{i \in N} \alpha_i \prod_{j=1}^{i-1} (1 - \alpha_j)$ is below a threshold $\tau_{opa}$, and checks depth error $E_{depth}(x,y)/D(x,y) > \tau_{depth}$ [S6]. New Gaussians are added to these regions, with depth images projected directly [S6].
 
 ### Point Densification
-A process in original 3DGS that adaptively controls the number of Gaussian points through clone and split operations to convert a sparse set into a denser one [S4]. It solves the problem of insufficient representation in complex or large-scale scenes by dynamically adjusting the density of Gaussians based on optimization progress [S4]. It sets itself apart from static initialization methods by allowing the Gaussian population to grow and shrink during training [S4].
+A process in original 3DGS that adaptively controls the number of Gaussian points through clone and split operations to convert a sparse set into a denser one [S7]. In HO-Gaussian, point densification is performed at regular intervals to provide new positions to the Gaussian pipeline, populating problematic regions [S7].
+
+### Naive Fine-tuning
+Naive generator fine-tuning method identical to model rewriting except freely optimizing all weights of the generator [S8]. It optimizes over 10,000 steps with a learning rate of $10^{-3}$ [S8].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The fine-tuning process is iterative, with each iteration involving rendering and gradient computation. The complexity is proportional to the number of Gaussians $N$ and the number of pixels $P$ in the rendered images, typically $O(N \cdot P)$ per iteration [S2, S3]. The total time depends on the number of iterations and the frequency of densification operations [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory usage is dominated by the storage of Gaussian attributes ($\mu, \Sigma, \alpha, \mathbf{c}$) and intermediate rendering buffers. The number of Gaussians can vary significantly during fine-tuning, with initial feed-forward methods potentially requiring more memory initially due to redundancy, which is reduced through pruning [S2].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Fine-tuning with pretrained weights (e.g., EVolSplat) can accelerate convergence and achieve photorealism comparable to or surpassing other methods [S2]. Rendering-guided densification (e.g., in SLAM) improves map quality and reduces forgetting [S3].
-*   **Scaling:** The method scales with the complexity of the scene, as densification operations increase the number of Gaussians in detailed regions [S4]. However, pruning helps control the overall number of primitives [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** Accelerates convergence, improves fidelity, handles sparse initialization, and reduces redundancy through pruning [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Can suffer from overfitting in continuous mapping scenarios [S3]. Requires careful tuning of hyperparameters (e.g., thresholds, learning rate) [S2, S3].
-*   **When to use:** When starting from sparse SfM points or pretrained weights, and when high fidelity and fast convergence are required [S2].
-*   **When not to use:** When computational resources are extremely limited and densification/pruning overhead is prohibitive [S2].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+*   **Scale-Adaptive (Mipmap-GS):** Converges within 1K iterations, using only 3% of the iterations required for training from scratch [S1]. Active pruning is performed every 100 iterations to keep the representation compact, reducing storage size, especially in zoom-out scenarios [S1].
+*   **Diffusion-Guided (FreeFix):** Uses pre-trained Image Diffusion Models (IDMs) to avoid the quadratic computational complexity of Video Diffusion Models (VDMs) [S3]. The interleaved 2D-3D refinement adds computational overhead per extrapolated view but avoids the cost of fine-tuning the diffusion backbone [S3].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+*   **Mipmap-GS:** On the NeRF Synthetic dataset at $\times 1/2$ zoom-out, achieves SSIM 0.971, PSNR 32.71, LPIPS 0.026, compared to 3DGS SSIM 0.951, PSNR 27.14, LPIPS 0.031 [S1]. On Mip-NeRF 360 at $\times 1/2$ zoom-out, achieves SSIM 0.854, PSNR 28.57, compared to 3DGS SSIM 0.774, PSNR 26.66 [S1]. Outperforms 3DGS in PSNR by an average of 9.25 dB for zoom-in and 10.40 dB for zoom-out [S1].
+*   **FreeFix:** Evaluated on LLFF, Mip-NeRF 360, and Waymo datasets using SDXL and Flux as backbone IDMs [S3]. Improves multi-frame consistency and achieves performance comparable to or surpassing fine-tuning-based methods [S3].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+*   **Strengths:** Mipmap-GS effectively solves aliasing artifacts (zoom-in spikes and zoom-out thickness) by deforming Gaussians rather than filtering [S1]. FreeFix improves extrapolated views without fine-tuning diffusion models, preserving generalization [S3].
+*   **Limitations:** Mipmap-GS requires constructing pseudo-GT on the fly, which adds rendering steps [S1]. FreeFix relies on the quality of the diffusion model and may introduce slight color biases that require correction [S3].
+
+**When to Use:**
+*   Use **Mipmap-GS** when rendering at arbitrary zoom scales is required and standard 3DGS exhibits aliasing [S1].
+*   Use **FreeFix** when dealing with sparse-view inputs or extrapolated views where artifacts are present, and fine-tuning diffusion models is computationally prohibitive [S3].
 
 ## Background
-Fine-tuning in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting has evolved from general deep learning transfer learning strategies, where lower layers are fixed and higher layers are optimized [S1]. In 3DGS, it specifically refers to the optimization phase where initial Gaussian primitives are refined to minimize rendering loss, often incorporating densification and pruning operations [S2]. The concept of densification and pruning was introduced in the original 3DGS paper to handle sparse initialization and adapt to scene complexity [Parent, S4]. Subsequent works have extended this by integrating rendering-guided densification for SLAM [S3], feed-forward initialization with fine-tuning [S2], and various regularization techniques to prevent overfitting [S3].
+Fine-tuning strategies for 3D Gaussian Splatting have emerged to address limitations in standard 3DGS, such as aliasing at varying scales [S1], poor generalization to OOD views [S1], and artifacts in sparse-view reconstruction [S3]. Mipmap-GS was introduced to solve the mismatch between fixed Gaussian distributions and varying pixel coverage at different zoom levels [S1]. FreeFix was developed to enhance extrapolated views using fine-tuning-free diffusion models, addressing the trade-off between generalization and fidelity [S3]. These methods build on the foundational 3DGS optimization and densification techniques [Parent].
 
